  "query_preview": "..\nA broad upper trough over the Gulf of Alaska will allow storm\nsystems to crash into British Columbia over the next several days\nwith drier/weaker systems emerging over the lee of the front range.\n\nThis will bring some instances of winds for northeast Montana while\ntemperatures are expected to remain above normal. The first weak \nfront will move in late tonight/Saturday. A wind gust to 58 mph \nhas a low confidence (30-50%) of occurring but have continued with\nthe previous High Wind Warning until it is proven to not occur.\n\nAlso issued a Lake Wind Advisory for Fort Peck Lake tomorrow from\n7 am till 4 pm as wind speeds greater than 20 mph could occur.\n\nAnother weak front moves in late Sunday afternoon with potentially\nbreezy conditions into the even but nowhere near as strong. \n\nRidging builds back into the Pacific Northwest Tuesday night and\nexpands throughout the week. This should keep conditions dry \nthrough the end of the work week but ensembles amplify this ridge \nheavily into Friday. Afterward confidence goes to low as ensemble \ngroups disagree on how to break the ridge down. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNo deviations from NBM were observed. \n\nHIGH confidence in the forecast through Monday night.\n\nMODERATE confidence through Wednesday night.\n\nLOW confidence Thursday onward.\n\nTFJ/GAH",
